          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Which conditions here require a second code, and does the order the two are listed in matter?",
          "answer": "Certain conditions require a second code in ICD-10 to provide more complete information about the diagnosis. This occurs in situations such as:\n\n1. **Code First / Use Additional Code**: These designations are used when there is an underlying condition and its manifestation. The underlying condition should be listed first.\n\n2. **Combination Codes**: These codes include two related conditions or a condition with a complication. When a combination code exists, it should replace the separate codes.\n\n3. **Conditions like Hypertension**: Hypertension becomes more complex when linked to other conditions like heart disease or kidney disease, necessitating the use of additional codes to specify the relationship and extent of these conditions.\n\n4. **Diabetes and its Complications**: Conditions such as diabetic chronic kidney disease require an additional code to indicate the severity or type of the complication.\n\nIn terms of order, yes, the order can matter. For example, when instructed to \"code first\" a certain condition, it must be documented before the associated one. Otherwise, \"code also\" allows for flexibility in sequencing.\n\nFor detailed guidance, reference can be made to the document on ICD-10-CM code structure and conventions.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
